course: biology
lesson: 365
title: Tiny champions: the strongest beetle, the highest-jumping flea and the leggiest millipede
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: The record breakers of the animal world for their size are not lions or elephants but beetles, fleas, bugs and mites, because being small changes the rules.
explain: Watch an ant drag a crumb bigger than itself: a muscle's strength depends on how thick it is, but weight depends on the whole body, so when an animal shrinks its weight falls much faster than its strength.
explain: That is why a beetle can pull a thousand times its weight while an elephant can only just carry a few people, and why a giant ant from a film would collapse under its own weight.
explain: Tiny animals also use tricks muscles alone cannot do: fleas and froghoppers store energy in springs and let it go like a catapult.
fact: A horned dung beetle was measured pulling about 1,141 times its own weight; males push and pull each other in narrow tunnels, and for a person it would be like pulling about six full double-decker buses.
fact: People often say rhinoceros beetles lift hundreds of times their weight; careful tests found less, but still many times their weight, and they use their horns to wrestle rivals off branches.
fact: A beetle's skeleton is on the outside, like light armour, with its muscles fixed inside it.
fact: Small animals are strong for their size because muscle strength grows with how thick a muscle is, but weight grows with the whole body; some ants can hold about 50 times their weight or more.
fact: A cat flea can jump about 30 centimetres, many times its body length, by storing energy in a rubbery pad and letting it go all at once like a catapult.
fact: An adult froghopper can leap about 70 centimetres up and may be the best high jumper of all for its size; its young hide in froth they blow from their own sticky liquid, which is not spit.
fact: Millipede means thousand feet, but most have fewer than 400 legs; in 2020 scientists found one in a deep drill hole in Australia with 1,306 legs, the first known with more than a thousand.
fact: Millipedes have two pairs of legs on most body sections and centipedes have one pair, which is how you tell them apart.
fact: Centipedes always have an odd number of pairs of legs, from 15 to over 190, so none has exactly 100 legs, and their front legs are fangs, so never pick up a big one.
fact: A tiger beetle in Australia runs about 2.5 metres a second, so fast its eyes cannot keep up, and it stops for a moment to look again.
fact: A tiny mite in California covers about 300 body lengths a second, more than any other animal measured.
fact: A water boatman about 2 millimetres long sings about as loudly as a motorbike close by, by rubbing a tiny part of its body, but most of the sound is lost passing from water into air.
fact: Fairyflies are tiny wasps, some smaller than a fifth of a millimetre, smaller than some creatures made of just one cell; they lay eggs inside other insects' eggs.
fact: Fairyflies have feathery wings of a stalk and long hairs and row through the air almost as if it were syrup.
exercise: About how many times its own weight was a horned dung beetle measured pulling? | About 1,141 times | Only about 2 times | About 10 times over | About a million times
exercise: Why do male dung beetles need to be so strong? | They push and pull rivals in narrow tunnels to win a home | They carry big lumps of elephant dung all the way up into the trees | They fight lions for food | They lift stones to build nests
exercise: What do careful tests say about rhinoceros beetles lifting hundreds of times their weight? | They lift less than that, but still many times their weight | They really do lift a thousand times their own weight, or even more than that | They cannot lift anything at all | They lift only with their horns
exercise: Where is a beetle's skeleton? | On the outside, like light armour | Inside its body, made of bones like ours | It has no skeleton at all | Only in its horn
exercise: Why are small animals strong for their size? | Strength grows with muscle thickness, but weight grows with the whole body | Small animals have far more muscles than big ones, hundreds more in every leg | Small animals eat stronger food | Small animals have no bones to carry
exercise: About how many times its weight can some ants hold? | About 50 times or more | About half of their own weight | Exactly 2 times, no more | About 100,000 times or more
exercise: How does a flea jump so far? | It stores energy in a rubbery pad and lets it go like a catapult | It flaps a pair of tiny hidden wings very fast as it takes off from the ground | It uses only very big leg muscles | It is blown by the wind
exercise: About how far can a cat flea jump? | About 30 centimetres | About 3 millimetres | About 30 metres | About 3 kilometres
exercise: What is the froth you find on garden plants in spring? | A hiding place a young froghopper makes from its own sticky liquid | Spit from a bird that flew past and stopped to clean its beak on the plant | Soap washed off a car | Eggs of a frog
exercise: About how high can an adult froghopper leap? | About 70 centimetres | About 7 millimetres | About 7 metres | It cannot jump
exercise: How many legs did the millipede found in Australia in 2020 have? | 1,306 | 1,000 exactly | 100 | 10,000
exercise: How can you tell a millipede from a centipede? | A millipede has two pairs of legs on most sections, a centipede one pair | A millipede has no legs at all and slides along on its slime like a slug | A centipede always has exactly 100 legs | A millipede has wings
exercise: Why does no centipede have exactly 100 legs? | Centipedes always have an odd number of pairs, and 50 pairs is even | Centipedes always have more than 1,000 legs, so 100 would be far too few for one | Centipedes lose legs on purpose | Centipedes have only 10 legs
exercise: Why should you never pick up a big centipede? | Its front legs are fangs that can bite | It will roll up into a tight ball and squash your hand | It sprays ink | It is too heavy
exercise: Why does a running tiger beetle stop for a moment? | It runs so fast its eyes cannot keep up, so it looks again | It gets tired out after just one step and has to sit down for a rest | It stops to eat | It forgets where it is going
exercise: Which animal covers the most body lengths a second of all measured? | A tiny mite in California | The cheetah, fastest of all land animals | The racehorse at full gallop | The house cat chasing a mouse
exercise: Why do people by the pond hardly hear the singing water boatman? | Most of the sound is lost passing from water into air | It sings only in the winter, when nobody is outside by the pond to hear it | Its song is too high for any ear | It sings only underground
exercise: How small are some fairyflies? | Smaller than a fifth of a millimetre | About the size of a honeybee or a wasp | About the size of a small house mouse | About a centimetre long, like a big ant
test: «a dung beetle pulls over a thousand times its weight, so a person-sized one would pull six buses lol» What is true? | No, if it grew to our size its weight would grow much faster than its strength | Yes, a big beetle would be even stronger for its size | Yes, all big animals are as strong as beetles | No, beetles have no muscles at all
test: In a monster film, a giant ant the size of a bus chases a city. What would really happen to an ant that big? | Its legs could hardly hold up its huge weight | It could lift 50 buses at once, because ants get stronger as they get bigger | It could jump over skyscrapers | It would be even faster than now
test: Count them: a girl finds a centipede with 15 pairs of legs. How many legs is that? | 30 | 15 | 100 | 45
test: Count them: three millipede sections each have two pairs of legs. How many legs on those three sections? | 12 | 6 | 3 | 24
test: «my mate found a centipede with exactly 50 pairs of legs» Why should you doubt him? | Centipedes always have an odd number of pairs, and 50 is even | Centipedes only have 4 pairs | All centipedes have exactly 1,000 legs | Centipedes have no legs, they slide
test: Why does a flea need a spring and not just strong muscles? | A spring lets go its energy faster than any muscle could | Fleas have no muscles at all anywhere in their bodies | Springs are lighter than legs | A spring helps it fly
test: In a cartoon, Jerry lifts a huge piano over his head and throws it at Tom. Which is true about tiny animals and lifting? | Small animals are strong for their size, but a mouse still cannot lift a piano | Mice can lift anything many times bigger than a house | Mice are weaker than ants for their size | Strength does not depend on size at all
test: Why might a soft young froghopper want to hide in froth? | To hide from hunters and stay damp | To keep itself warm when snow falls in the middle of summer | To catch small flies in it like a web | To help the plant it sits on grow faster
test: Why would a tiger beetle run in short bursts instead of one long sprint? | It must stop to see again, because its eyes cannot keep up | It runs out of legs | It only likes running downhill, and has to stop at the bottom of every slope | Sand makes it slow down
test: «insects are just small, nothing tiny is a record breaker» Which tiny animal breaks the record for body lengths a second? | A tiny mite in California | No tiny animal, only the cheetah | The elephant on the savannah | The tortoise, slow but steady
test: Why might ordinary flat wings not work for a fairyfly smaller than a speck of dust? | At that size air feels thick like syrup, so it rows with hairy wings | Flat wings are too heavy for any insect | Fairyflies live underwater | Flat wings are only for birds
test: Why would an elephant never be as strong for its size as a beetle? | Its huge weight grows faster than its muscle strength | Elephants never use their muscles, so they get weaker | Elephants have weaker bones than beetles | Elephants are lazy
exam: Identify the principal reason for which small animals are capable of lifting loads many times their own weight. | Muscular strength increases with the cross-section of muscle, whereas weight increases with the whole volume | Small animals possess a greater number of muscles than large animals | Small animals consume exceptionally nourishing food | Small animals are devoid of any skeleton
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the leaping of the cat flea? | Energy is stored in an elastic pad and released suddenly in the manner of a catapult | The flea employs its wings to rise into the air | The leap is effected solely by large muscles of the legs | The flea is propelled by currents of air
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the number of legs possessed by centipedes. | The number of pairs is invariably odd, so that no centipede possesses exactly one hundred legs | Every centipede possesses exactly one hundred legs | Every centipede possesses in excess of one thousand legs | The number of pairs is invariably even
exam: State the number of legs possessed by the millipede discovered in a deep drill hole in Australia in the year 2020. | One thousand three hundred and six | Seven hundred and fifty, the same as every millipede | One hundred | Ten thousand
